# PTM double signature (rule 241)

Source: the internal memo of 2026-10-17 (M1.1–M1.3) and the owner's decisions of 2026-11-17, recorded in
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-comprehensive-closure-1.md`. Delivered in PRs; each PR adds its section. Builds on rule 237 §14 (one valid consent per visit,
supervised void, signatures on the clinical evidence disk).

## 0. Owner decisions (CG-22)

- The PTM carries TWO signatures: the patient (or the person consenting) signs through the front office; the treating
  doctor signs through the doctor's own screens. Both will be required before RME and odontogram writing (PR2, not
  yet). "Mulai Pemeriksaan" stays before consent. The audit trail and the supervised void (rule 237 §14.2) are kept.
  Historical consents are never changed.
- Plan defaults accepted on 2026-11-17, including permission `countersign_rme_consents` for Doctor and the insert-only
  countersignature record.

## 1. The doctor's countersignature — capture and storage (PR1; no gate change)

1. **Its own record.** `trx_rme_visit_consent_doctor_signatures` (additive migration): `rme_visit_consent_id`
   (UNIQUE), `doctor_id`, `signed_by` (the account), `signature_path` (private object key, `$hidden`),
   `signature_sha256` (of the stored bytes), `signed_at` (clinic time, `ClinicalClock`, as the consent's own
   `signed_at`), `created_at`; every foreign key `restrictOnDelete`; `down()` refuses while rows exist. The model
   `RmeVisitConsentDoctorSignature` throws on update and on delete. The consent row is never updated (AIR-RMC-12);
   `RmeVisitConsent::doctorCountersignature()` / `isDoctorCountersigned()` read the record.
2. **The legacy drawing is not the step.** The optional doctor drawing on the capture form
   (`trx_rme_visit_consents.doctor_signature_path`) is display history: it is not bound to an account, it never counts
   as the countersignature, and no consent is countersigned retroactively.
3. **The one writer.** `RmeVisitConsentService::countersign(RmeVisitConsent, User, array)`:
   - checks `countersign_rme_consents` FIRST (CG-06; the route and the policy ask before it), so an account without
     it never has its payload parsed; then decodes `doctor_signature` with `PrescriptionCanvasDecoder` and
     `MAX_SIGNATURE_PAYLOAD_LENGTH` (the rule 228 §10 / rule 237 §5 bounds the patient's signature has): missing,
     blank, oversized, over-declared or non-PNG is refused before anything is written;
   - inside one transaction: locks the visit row FIRST (`lockForStatusDecision`, FOR NO KEY UPDATE, rule 223 §6 — the
     lock and order of `sign()`, `void()` and `transitionStatus()`), re-reads the consent, drops the request's memoised
     doctor identity (`DoctorIdentityResolver::forget()`) so a deactivation or unlink committed meanwhile is seen, then
     decides. Every AUTHORIZATION guard runs before any STATE guard, each with its own message (403,
     `AuthorizationException`): permission (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_PERMISSION`); the consent's visit exists
     (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_ENCOUNTER`); branch and patient scope (`MESSAGE_OUT_OF_SCOPE`); the encounter authority every
     clinical writer uses (`actorMayWorkOnEncounter`: never the front-office tier, a clinical role,
     `manage_clinic_visits`, working branch, doctor room, doctor-patient scope; `MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_ENCOUNTER`);
     identity — a linked doctor (`DoctorIdentityResolver`, `mst_doctors.user_id`; `MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_NOT_LINKED`),
     active (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_DOCTOR_INACTIVE`), equal to the locked visit's `doctor_id`
     (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_NOT_VISIT_DOCTOR`) and to the consent's `doctor_id` (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_NOT_CONSENT_DOCTOR`).
     Then the state guards (`ValidationException` on `consent`): visit `in_progress` (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_WINDOW`); the
     consent is the visit's live consent (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_VOIDED`); maker ≠ checker — not the account that captured
     the patient signature (`signed_by`; `MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_SELF`); not yet countersigned
     (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_DUPLICATE`). Identity, not role, so a Super Admin, Owner or Supervisor RME that is not that
     doctor is refused although `Gate::before` passes them through every policy. A doctor re-placed on the visit after
     the patient signed cannot countersign until a void and a new patient signature;
   - writes the object to `ClinicalEvidenceStorage` under `rme-consents/{consent_number}/doctor-countersign-<16 hex>.png`
     (fresh random key, rule 224 §3), then inserts the row inside a savepoint: a UNIQUE violation (the only unique key
     besides the id) becomes `MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_DUPLICATE`;
   - audits one `RME_CONSENT_DOCTOR_SIGNED` row on `trx_rme_visit_consents` with `clinic_visit_id`, `doctor_id` and
     `doctor_signature_id` only — no name, path or hash;
   - removes only the object this call wrote when the body fails before it finished (rule 224 §1a); never a committed
     object.
4. **HTTP.** `rme.consents.countersign.create` (GET, `cache.headers:private;no_store;max_age=0` — it renders the
   document) and `rme.consents.countersign` (POST), both `permission:countersign_rme_consents`. `{consent}` is
   constrained by the global `DatabaseRouteKey` pattern; no per-route `where` (AIR-RMC-37).
   `RmeVisitConsentPolicy::countersign` (permission, working branch, not voided, visit `in_progress`, doctor-patient
   scope, then the service's unlocked `assertCountersignable()`: an `AuthorizationException` denies; a state refusal,
   reachable only after every authorization guard passed, allows so the operator learns why);
   `CountersignRmeVisitConsentRequest` authorizes before it validates (R8A-2) and accepts only `doctor_signature`
   (already in `dontFlash`, rule 237 §3; never flashed back). An out-of-branch or other-doctor request answers 403,
   consistent with the consent `view` (rule 237 §14.6); an unknown or non-canonical id answers 404. A state refusal
   on the GET form redirects to the consent page with an error toast; on the POST it is a field error on `consent`.
5. **Entry points.** Ruang Perawatan (`rme.treatment-room-worklist.index`) shows "Tanda Tangani PTM" on a row only when
   `RmeVisitConsentService::countersignableConsentsFor()` (called by the controller, never Blade) returns that visit's
   consent; the consent page shows the same button when `canCountersign()` is true. Both are presentation; the route,
   policy and service decide.
6. **Rendering.** The controller passes the countersignature record, its image (inline data URI, rule 121) and
   `countersignaturePending()` (live consent, visit `in_progress`, none recorded — whoever views). The document's
   doctor box adds, after the legacy optional drawing (which always renders exactly as before, also beside a
   countersignature), the countersignature labelled "Tanda tangan dokter (PTM)" with the doctor's name and
   "dd/mm/YYYY HH:mm WITA" — or "Gambar tanda tangan dokter tidak tersedia." when its object is unreadable — and, only
   while pending, "Belum ditandatangani dokter". A historical consent (no countersignature, nothing pending: finished
   or cancelled visit, voided consent) renders byte for byte as before rule 241 (the additions use column-0
   directives and no Blade comments, so they emit nothing; pinned against a copy of the old partial in
   `tests/Fixtures/rme-consent-document-pre-rule-241.blade.php.txt`). Never an empty image (rule 224 §7). The consent
   page's trail shows the PTM doctor row only when a countersignature exists or is pending.
7. **Unchanged in PR1 (history; superseded by §2–§4 in PR2).** Every authoring gate (`assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()`, the pre-consent odontogram
   authority of rule 134, performed treatments, "Selesai Pemeriksaan") still needs only the patient's valid consent;
   payment never consults consent (AIR-RMC-11); the supervised void works exactly as before and keeps a voided
   consent's countersignature row; the capture form, its optional doctor drawing and the Doctor role's
   `manage_rme_consents` are untouched (PR2).
8. **Deploy.** `php artisan migrate --force` (additive) after the runner's backup, then `PermissionSeeder`, then
   `RoleSeeder`, then `permission:cache-reset` (CG-15).
9. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/RmeVisitConsentDoctorCountersignTest.php` (token `RmeVisitConsent`, declared in
   `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`). A multi-process PostgreSQL race suite is not part of PR1: the visit lock order and
   the UNIQUE backstop are proven in-process.

## 2. Both signatures gate clinical WRITING (PR2)

1. **One predicate.** `RmeVisitConsentService::hasExecutedConsent(ClinicVisit)`: the visit's live consent
   (`validConsentFor()`) carries its doctor countersignature (§1). `ptmStateFor()` names the three states
   `PTM_AWAITING_PATIENT` (no live consent), `PTM_AWAITING_DOCTOR` (live consent, no countersignature) and
   `PTM_EXECUTED`; `ptmStatesFor()` answers a page of visits in one query. Persisted evidence only (AIR-RMC-10);
   `hasValidConsent()` keeps meaning "the patient signed" and gates nothing on its own any more.
2. **The single authorities, extended — no per-caller checks.**
   - `assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()` (and `canAuthorRmeForPatient()` / `resolveAuthorizedEncounter()`):
     condition 4 is the EXECUTED consent; inside a write transaction it is re-decided on the visit row locked FIRST.
     Covers sheet create/update/finalize, handwriting pages (also edit-in-place), structured diagnoses, the diagnosis
     override (`MESSAGE_RME_AWAITING_PATIENT` / `MESSAGE_RME_AWAITING_DOCTOR`).
   - `assertPerformedTreatmentAuthoringAllowed()` and `RmePerformedTreatmentService`'s locked re-check: executed
     (`RmePerformedTreatmentRefusal::CONSENT_REQUIRED`).
   - `assertOdontogramAuthoringAllowed()`: condition 4 added (supersedes rule 134 for WRITING); inside a write
     transaction it locks the visit row FIRST, re-checks `in_progress` and the executed consent on it, and only then
     does `OdontogramService` lock (`findByClinicVisitForUpdate`) or write the chart
     (`MESSAGE_ODONTOGRAM_AWAITING_PATIENT` / `_DOCTOR`).
   - "Selesai Pemeriksaan": `ClinicVisitService::transitionStatus()` calls `assertExecutedConsentForFinish()` before the
     lock and on the locked row (`MESSAGE_FINISH_AWAITING_PATIENT` / `_DOCTOR`, key `status`).
3. **Unchanged.** "Mulai Pemeriksaan" stays BEFORE consent; signing stays `in_progress` only (§2a of rule 109); READING
   — the workspace, the odontogram page, history, legacy archives — is never gated; prescriptions stay outside the gate
   (rule 109 §2c, rule 226); payment never consults consent (AIR-RMC-11); the supervised void (rule 237 §14.2) is
   unchanged and REOPENS the gate: after a void both signatures are needed again on the new consent. Super Admin is
   bound: the gate is the consent, not a permission.

## 3. The UI names the PTM state (PR2; rule 135 mirror; MEMO-F5)

1. Every fact is decided in a controller, never by a service call in Blade: `ClinicVisitController::show()`
   (`ptmState`, `visitConsent`, `examinationConsentExecuted`, `countersignConsent`, `countersignRefusal`),
   `patientQueue()` and `roomWorklist()` (`ptmStates`; Ruang Perawatan also `countersignableConsents` and
   `countersignSelfCaptured`), `MedicalRecordController` (`activeEncounterPtmState`,
   `activeEncounterCountersignConsent`, `activeEncounterCountersignRefusal`, `clinicVisitConsentExecuted`),
   `OdontogramController::show()` (`odontogramPtmBlock` from `odontogramPtmBlockFor()`, the countersign link and
   refusal, `odontogramIsLiveEncounter`), `RmeVisitConsentController::show()` (`countersignRefusal`).
2. Detail Kunjungan shows awaiting patient (front office: "Pilih Form Consent"), "Menunggu tanda tangan dokter" (the
   treating doctor: "Tanda Tangani PTM") or complete; "Selesai Pemeriksaan" is offered only when executed. Antrian
   Pasien and Ruang Perawatan show a PTM badge on every in-progress row (`rme.visits.partials.ptm-state-badge`). The RME
   workspace (populated and empty) and the odontogram page show the honest state instead of a submittable form
   (`rme.visits.partials.ptm-authoring-banner`); reading stays open.
3. When the only obstacle is that the doctor's own account captured the patient signature
   (`MESSAGE_COUNTERSIGN_SELF`, which names the remedy: a supervised void and a new capture by the front office), Ruang
   Perawatan, Detail Kunjungan, the RME workspace, the odontogram page and the consent page say so
   (`RmeVisitConsentService::countersignRefusal()` / `countersignOutlookFor()`).
4. Copy that was false is corrected: the capture form and the consent page no longer say payment waits for consent.

## 4. Who captures the patient signature (PR2; owner default 1)

1. `RoleSeeder`: the Doctor role loses `manage_rme_consents` (it keeps `view_rme_consents` and
   `countersign_rme_consents`); Front Office, Kasir and Admin Klinik capture, Supervisor RME is the fallback. RoleSeeder
   syncs role permissions, so the deploy removes it from the role (a direct grant on an account is untouched).
2. The front-office capture form offers no doctor canvas any more. The `doctor_signature` field of the store request
   and `sign()` stay bounded for older callers; a legacy optional drawing never counts as the countersignature (§1.2).
3. Deploy: `PermissionSeeder` → `RoleSeeder` → `permission:cache-reset` (CG-15).

## 5. Compatibility (PR2)

No data changes and no migration. A visit `in_progress` at deploy with only a patient consent reads as AWAITING DOCTOR:
what is written stays and stays readable; further writes and finishing wait for the treating doctor's countersignature
(the legacy optional drawing is not account-bound, so it is not grandfathered). Historical consents are never
countersigned afterwards (§1.6 renders them as before). Production is a testing database (CLAUDE.md), so no operational
encounter is disrupted.

Tests: `tests/Feature/RME/RmeVisitConsentTwoSignatureGateTest.php` (token `RmeVisitConsent`) and
`tests/Feature/RME/RmeOdontogramTwoSignatureGateTest.php` (token `Odontogram`), both declared in
`critical_gate_mandatory_suites`. The shared fixture `rmeSignedConsentFor()` now means an EXECUTED consent (it adds the
countersignature row through `rmeDoctorCountersignatureFor()`); gate tests build their state only through the services.
